A polychrome sprite can draw some of its sides hard, for a face cut into
patches that meet edge to edge: the canvas's nine-slice images.

Every polychrome sprite fades its alpha over the half pixel inside each
side (`saturate(0.5 - distance)` on the quad SDF), which smooths a lone
quad's edge. Nine patches of one face share six inner sides, and there the
fade costs alpha on both sides of the seam: a pixel whose center falls
within half a pixel of it is drawn once, at 50 to 100 percent, so every
seam showed as a faint line wherever it didn't land on a pixel boundary.
On a tilted face no seam does.

`paint_image_patch` is `paint_image_quad` with `inner: [bool; 4]` (left,
top, right, bottom), and `paint_image_quad` calls it with none. The sprite
carries the sides as a bitmask in the slot `pad2` held, so its layout
doesn't move on any backend. The fragment stage measures the fade from the
soft sides only. Rasterization covers a pixel whose center lies on a
shared side once, so two patches built from the same corners leave neither
a gap nor a doubled line. Blade (wgsl) and DirectX 11 (hlsl) both. The
Metal renderer is compiled out (rox builds gpui with `macos-blade`), and
its header would be regenerated from the Rust struct anyway.

Not droppable on a gpui bump: no release has a perspective image face.

Diffed against the tree with every earlier patch applied.

